 ## 6. Known Issues
 
@@ -165,12 +167,16 @@ Not present: any module other than `identity`, `appService`, `incidents`, `alert
 
 35. **SLICE-10 interpretation choices where the slice is silent.** (a) Keys the model adds beyond the schema are dropped, not rejected; an empty `evidence` array is valid. (b) A job whose payload `incidentId` is not the alert's (or the alert has none) is non-retryable and ends `FAILED` before any provider call. (c) An alert with all four fields empty is still sent. (d) `rawOutput` is stored on each invalid attempt (the parsed JSON, or the text as a JSON string; overwritten by the next attempt) and on success; never logged or exposed. (e) If the provider reports no `modelVersion`, the requested model id is stored. (f) Errors the slice does not name as final are retryable, including a provider `4xx` other than 401/403/invalid key (a wrong model id is a `404` and is retried until exhaustion). (g) Invalid model output is thrown as a plain `InvalidModelOutputError`, so SLICE-08 classes it `UNKNOWN_INTERNAL` (retryable) and the dead letter's `lastError` reads `InvalidModelOutputError`; no reason was added to `src/infra/jobs/errors.ts`. (h) Parallel jobs for one alert may each call the provider (the schema has no claim state and none was added); only one result is stored, one audit event and one notification. A `FAILED` row written by a parallel job does not stop another job from storing a valid result. (i) An unknown alert creates no row (foreign key), so it is only dead-lettered; a database failure before the row exists cannot write `FAILED` either, and the API keeps showing `PENDING` until a re-drive. If writing `FAILED` itself fails, that is logged and the original error is rethrown. (j) A re-driven `FAILED` job goes back to `PENDING` and can succeed. (k) The prompt text (`triage.prompt.ts`) is not specified by the design; it asks for exactly the validated JSON object and tells the model to treat the alert fields as data. (l) The AI module reads the four `Alert` columns directly in its own repository, as SLICE-06 reads `AppService`; no `alerts` export was added.
 
+36. **SLICE-11 decisions made by the project owner.** (a) Error precedence for P2/P3/P4 (the slice's table and text disagreed): no postmortem row (incident not yet resolved) → `404 POSTMORTEM_NOT_FOUND`; a row that is not an unreviewed `DRAFT` (generating, failed, already reviewed, including every closed incident and a second approve) → `409 POSTMORTEM_STATE_INVALID`; only then an incident that is not `RESOLVED` → `403 INVALID_ACTION` (not reachable in practice, kept as a guard). P4 and P5 follow the slice: anything but (`RESOLVED`, `FAILED`) → `403 INVALID_ACTION`, and P5 without a row is `403`. (b) The worker's repository (`ai/postmortem.repository.ts`) reads `Incident`, `Comment` and `Postmortem` and writes `Postmortem` directly, as SLICE-10 does for `Alert`; there is no `ai → incidents` import and no lint change. **Consequence:** if a module is ever extracted into a service, this table access must be replaced by an interface exported from the owning module's `index.ts`. (c) After committing `FAILED` and the warning notification the processor always rethrows, so the outbox row is `DEAD_LETTER` and a dead-letter job exists; re-driving it is a no-op (state is `FAILED`), recovery is P4 or P5. (d) `prompt` stores the instruction text and `inputData` the JSON of the exact flat record sent to the provider; the prompt wording is mine (`postmortem.prompt.ts`), never logged or exposed.
+
+37. **SLICE-11 interpretation choices where the slice is silent.** (a) The provider input is a flat record, so `comments` is an array of strings, each the JSON `{ "title", "description" }` of one comment (title may be `null`), ordered by `createdAt`, `id`. (b) Request texts are trimmed before the 1–20,000 check (as the other DTOs); model output follows the same rule; `unknowns` must be present as a string (1–20,000) or `null`, an empty string is invalid output (retried, then `OUTPUT_INVALID`). (c) On P3/P5 the chain gate runs before the `404 POSTMORTEM_NOT_FOUND`/state checks (which need the locks), so a caller outside the chain gets `403 FORBIDDEN` first. (d) The `409` details are `[{ currentGenerationStatus, currentReviewStatus }]`. (e) Worker: a missing postmortem, a payload whose `incidentId` does not match, or a `RESOLVED` incident without `resolvedTimestamp` is non-retryable and writes nothing; a provider `CONFIGURATION` error is final and ends as `AI_UNAVAILABLE`, like any other non-provider failure on the last attempt. (f) The worker locks the incident `FOR SHARE` before the postmortem `FOR UPDATE` (the kernel locks the incident first) so a parallel approve cannot deadlock with a completing job. (g) P2 runs through the kernel with an empty plan, so edits and approvals serialize on the two row locks and a lock timeout is `409 CONCURRENCY_CONFLICT`. (h) The provider-error mapping moved from `triage.processor.ts` into `provider-job-error.ts` (behavior unchanged, triage tests pass) so both processors share it. (i) The failure notification's `occurrenceId` is the request's outbox event id, so each P4 retry that fails again notifies again. (j) The model's `riskLevel` is stored as is; `mttrMinutes` is computed once at generation (and at P5), not recomputed at approval.
